From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kevin Cui <bh@bugs.cc>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 23:37:42 -0400
Subject: fix: follow the live credential's owner when the config names another
 slot

`~/.claude.json`'s `oauthAccount` is what picks the active slot, but every
Claude Code sharing that config writes it. A long-lived process that still
holds the previous account in memory rewrites it after a `cswap switch`
(measured: the Chrome extension's native host, started before the switch,
rewrote the block 16 seconds after cswap had written the new account), and
an install that authenticates on its own can do the same. The config then
names managed slot B while the credential store still holds slot A's token.
Trusting the label costs both accounts:

- A is treated as idle, so its backup grant (a generation A's own rotation
  already consumed) is POSTed, gets `invalid_grant`, and A is quarantined
  with a false "re-login needed" although its live credential is healthy.
- B is served A's foreign credential (429s, the foreign sentinel) and never
  fetches from its own, refreshable backup.

Neither account refreshes, and the display blames A.

`_build_accounts_info` now attributes the live bytes before picking the
active slot: by refresh-lineage fingerprint against every slot backup
first (offline, the common case), then from the verdicts earlier passes
persisted in `cache/lineage_owners.json`, and only then by the profile
oracle, which needs a fresh access token. Persisting the verdict is what
makes this work for `cswap ls`: it is a new process every time, and Claude
Code refreshes the live token only when it is used, so on a machine where
the day's work runs through a Claude Code that never touches the credential
store (the desktop app passes its own token in the environment) the token
spends most of its life expired and the oracle cannot be asked. A verdict
recorded while the token was fresh keeps attributing the lineage until it
rotates (the refresh-token hash is the lineage), and the next fresh pass
records the successor. Affirmative verdicts are fed into `_probe_verdicts`
for the owner slot, which licenses the locked refresh path to POST the live
grant and the fresh-path resync to adopt it without a second probe.

Any doubt (no access token, an expired token with no recorded verdict, an
unreachable oracle, a lineage no managed slot owns) falls back to the
config slot, i.e. the previous behavior. `_live_identity_matches` accepts
the owner slot while the config still carries the stale identity it had at
attribution time, so the locked refresh and resync paths work for it.

An owner whose backup is struck (the shape above) is also healed on the
spot: an attributed live lineage is resynced into the backup right away,
because the quarantine would otherwise keep the slot from ever reaching the
fast-path resync that normally does this, leaving it at "re-login needed"
forever.

The switch-display test's in-memory live store now also backs
`_read_active_credentials`; its live file never followed the switch, and the
outgoing account's token left in it is now (correctly) attributed to the
outgoing slot.

